<p>After a storm or a burst pipe, waiting is hard. The roof is tarped, the drywall is cut, and every week without a decision means more disruption. A <strong>delayed insurance claim in San Antonio</strong> can happen for ordinary reasons, like catastrophe-level claim volume after a hailstorm, and for frustrating ones, like repeated document requests, rotating adjusters, or estimates that sit for weeks in review. Texas law sets general timelines for how insurers must handle claims. Knowing them, and documenting every step, can help you keep a slow claim moving.</p>

<h2>Texas Prompt Payment of Claims Deadlines</h2>
<p>The Texas Prompt Payment of Claims statute, <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542, Subchapter B</a>, sets general deadlines for many first-party property claims. Here is a simplified overview:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>General deadline</th><th>Statute</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
