<tr><td>Accept or reject the claim in writing</td><td>15 business days after receiving all requested items</td><td>§542.056</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extension if the insurer needs more time</td><td>Insurer must notify you and give reasons; then up to 45 more days</td><td>§542.056</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay the claim after accepting it</td><td>5 business days after notifying you of acceptance</td><td>§542.057</td></tr>
<tr><td>Declared weather catastrophe</td><td>Deadlines generally extended by 15 days</td><td>§542.059</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>These are general rules. Eligible surplus lines insurers have different timeframes (30 business days), some claim types are handled differently, and the details of when a clock starts can be disputed. Treat this table as a practical guide for tracking your claim, not as legal advice.</p>

<h2>Why San Antonio Claims Get Delayed</h2>
<h3>Catastrophe volume</h3>
<p>Bexar County hail season peaks in April and May. After a major event, such as the April 12, 2016 hailstorm across northwest San Antonio or the May 3, 2021 storm that produced hail up to 5.5 inches near Sayers and China Grove, insurers can receive thousands of claims in a few days. Winter events cause similar surges; Winter Storm Uri in February 2021 led to burst pipes across the city, and ice storms in 2022, 2025, and 2026 brought more. Our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page lists notable local events.</p>
<h3>Rotating adjusters</h3>
<p>Large claim volumes often mean independent or catastrophe adjusters from outside the area. When a file changes hands, notes and photos may not transfer cleanly, and a new adjuster may ask for information you already sent.</p>
<h3>Repeated information requests</h3>
<p>Insurers may request recorded statements, proof of loss forms, receipts, contractor estimates, or engineering inspections. Some requests are reasonable; others repeat earlier requests. The decision clock generally runs from receipt of the items requested, so documentation matters.</p>
<h3>Engineer and expert reports</h3>
<p>Roof, foundation, and water claims sometimes wait on an engineer or other expert, which can add weeks.</p>
<h3>Disputes over scope</h3>
<p>Sometimes the claim is not stalled so much as stuck in disagreement. If the insurer has made a decision you disagree with, see <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> or <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>.</p>

