<h2>Proof of Loss, Recorded Statements, and Other Requests</h2>
<p>Many delays come down to paperwork. A proof of loss is a sworn statement of the amount you are claiming, and many policies require one when the insurer asks for it, often within a set number of days. Read the request carefully, note the deadline, and ask for the form in writing if it was not provided. If you do not yet know the full amount, say so and explain what you are still waiting on.</p>
<p>Insurers may also ask for a recorded statement or an examination under oath, receipts for emergency repairs, contents inventories, or access to the property for another inspection. Keep copies of everything you send, note the date it was delivered, and confirm receipt by email. When an insurer asks again for something you already provided, reply with the date and method you sent it. That record helps show when the insurer had what it needed, which matters for the Chapter 542 timelines. If a request seems unusual or you are uncomfortable with it, it is reasonable to ask for an explanation in writing and to consult a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>Steps to Take When Your Claim Is Delayed</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Put it in writing.</strong> Follow up phone calls with an email summarizing what was said, by whom, and when.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a claim log.</strong> Record every call, email, inspection, and document you send, with dates.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for a written list of outstanding items.</strong> Ask the adjuster exactly what is still needed to make a decision.</li>
<li><strong>Respond completely and promptly.</strong> Send requested documents in one organized package and keep proof of delivery.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for a supervisor.</strong> If your adjuster is unresponsive, ask in writing for the name of the supervisor or claim manager.</li>
<li><strong>Keep protecting the property.</strong> Your duty to prevent further damage continues while you wait. Keep receipts for emergency repairs.</li>
<li><strong>Consider a TDI complaint.</strong> The <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> accepts complaints about claim handling.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> can help you organize what the insurer will likely ask for.</p>

<h2>How a San Antonio Public Adjuster Can Help With Delays</h2>
<p>A public adjuster in San Antonio can take over day-to-day communication with the insurer, organize and submit a complete claim package, track the Chapter 542 timelines, follow up in writing, and attend re-inspections. Often, a delay ends once the insurer has a clear, complete, well-documented claim in hand, including an Xactimate estimate it can compare with its own. We cannot guarantee how quickly an insurer will act. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>When to Talk to an Attorney</h2>
<p>Texas law provides remedies for some prompt payment violations, and suits over weather-related claims require written notice at least 61 days before filing under Chapter 542A. Many policies also set a contractual deadline to sue. Public adjusters are not attorneys and do not give legal advice, so if your claim has been delayed for a long time, if you suspect bad faith, or if a deadline may be approaching, consult a Texas attorney.</p>

